      },
      "text": "## Layer 1 — Enumerative: How Many?"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#012",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:combinatorics:00-overview, git-history:combinatorics:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "combinatorics/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "combinatorics/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:combinatorics:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "combinatorics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "combinatorics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Combinatorics - Landscape Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The starting point. You have a set of configurations and you want its\ncardinality. The discipline is to **never count by listing** when you can count\nby structure."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#013",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:combinatorics:00-overview, git-history:combinatorics:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "combinatorics/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "combinatorics/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:combinatorics:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "combinatorics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "combinatorics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Combinatorics - Landscape Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n|  ENUMERATIVE TOOLKIT (in rough order of power)                |\n|                                                               |\n|  Sum / product rule .......... disjoint cases / sequences     |\n|  Permutations / combinations . ordered / unordered selection  |\n|  The twelvefold way .......... balls-in-boxes, all 12 cells   |\n|  Inclusion-exclusion ......... overcount, then correct        |\n|  Generating functions ........ encode the whole sequence      |\n|  Recurrences ................. count_n in terms of count_<n   |\n|  Bijection ................... THE master move; reduce to     |\n|                                a count you already know       |\n+---------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#014",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:combinatorics:00-overview, git-history:combinatorics:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "combinatorics/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "combinatorics/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:combinatorics:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "combinatorics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "combinatorics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Combinatorics - Landscape Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The crown jewel of technique is the **bijective proof**: to show two sequences\nare equal, exhibit an explicit, invertible map between the objects they count.\nA bijective proof is the gold standard — it explains *why* equal, not merely\n*that* equal. Algebraic identities (Vandermonde, hockey-stick) almost always\nhave a bijective or combinatorial reading, and finding it is the point."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#015",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:combinatorics:00-overview, git-history:combinatorics:00-overview]",
        "canonical_path": "combinatorics/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "concepts": "[overview]",
        "current_path": "combinatorics/00-OVERVIEW.md",
        "id": "maxim:combinatorics:overview",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "combinatorics",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[overview]",
        "section": "combinatorics",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Combinatorics - Landscape Overview",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Old → new bridge.** If you have written recursive descent or analyzed an\nalgorithm's running time, you have already solved recurrences and counted paths\nthrough a state space. Combinatorics formalizes that instinct: a recurrence is a\nrecursive subproblem decomposition, and its solution (closed form or asymptotic)\nis exactly the kind of bound you put in a complexity analysis."
    },
    {
      "id": "00-OVERVIEW.md#016",
      "path": [
        "00-OVERVIEW.md"
